{
  "moduleId": "m00236",
  "title": {
    "segmentId": "m00236:title:auto-1",
    "text": "Chemistry of Nitriles"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "8acc76f4-3b16-4137-90af-819f2966fe48"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "In nitrile, carbon is triple-bonded to nitrogen and single-bonded to R. In acid, carbon has a bond to R, a double bond to oxygen, and a single bond to hydroxyl."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "The structures of Cyanocycline A and Lotaustralin. Lotaustralin is a cyanogenic glycoside with an acetal carbon (C with two R groups and two O R groups)."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00001",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00236:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "Preparation of Nitriles"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00003",
          "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00003"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00003",
          "class": "unnumbered",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00003-alt",
              "text": "The  reaction of 2-ethylhexanamide with thionyl chloride in benzene at 80 degrees Celsius forms 2-ethylhexanenitrile (94 percent yield), sulfur dioxide, and two equivalents of hydrogen chloride."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00004",
          "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00004"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00004",
          "class": "unnumbered",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00004-alt",
              "text": "Reaction mechanism between amide and thionyl chloride. Nitrogen pushes electrons to carbonyl oxygen, results in O S bond. Subsequent loss of chlorine, deprotonation of N, loss of C O bond."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00005",
          "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00005"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00002",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00236:title:sect-00002-title",
        "text": "Reactions of Nitriles"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00006",
          "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00006"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00005",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00005-alt",
              "text": "Nucleophilic attack on carbon of carbonyl compound forms carbanion and further forms products. Nucleophilic attack on nitrile carbon along with its ball-and-stick model forming imine ion and further forms products."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_005.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00007",
          "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00007"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00006",
          "class": "scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00236:caption:fig-00006-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00006-alt",
              "text": "The conversion of nitriles to amides and carboxylic acids using water. Reduction of nitriles to amines using lithium aluminum hydride. The reaction of nitriles with Grignard reagent to give ketones."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_006.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00003",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00236:title:sect-00003-title",
            "text": "Hydrolysis: Conversion of Nitriles into Carboxylic Acids"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00008",
              "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00008"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00007",
              "class": "unnumbered",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00007-alt",
                  "text": "Hydrolysis of nitriles to amide using acidic or basic aqueous solutions. Amide is hydrolyzed to carboxylic acid and ammonia using acidic or basic aqueous solutions."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_007.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00009",
              "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00009"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00008",
              "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
              "caption": {
                "segmentId": "m00236:caption:fig-00008-caption"
              },
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00008-alt",
                  "text": "Base-catalyzed mechanism of nitrile hydrolysis to carboxylate anion via four reversible steps: nucleophilic addition of hydroxide to form imine anion, protonation to imine, tautomerization to amide, hydrolysis to carboxylate ion."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_008.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00010",
              "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00010"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00009",
              "class": "unnumbered",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00009-alt",
                  "text": "Nucleophilic attack on carbonyl carbon by hydroxide pushes electron density onto carbonyl oxygen; regenerating carbon-oxygen double bond expels amide ion. Proton abstraction by amide ions forms carboxylate ion and ammonia."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_009.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00004",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00236:title:sect-00004-title",
            "text": "Reduction: Conversion of Nitriles into Amines"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00011",
              "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00011"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00010",
              "class": "unnumbered",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00010-alt",
                  "text": "Hydride ion from lithium aluminum hydride adds twice to nitrile, forming an imine and a dianion. Intermediate anions contain nitrogen bonded to aluminum chloride. Further, hydrolysis converts benzonitrile to benzylamine."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_010.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00005",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00236:title:sect-00005-title",
            "text": "Reaction of Nitriles with Grignard Reagents"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00012",
              "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00012"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00011",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00011-alt",
                  "text": "Nucleophilic addition of Grignard reagent on nitrile carbon forms imine anion (intermediate). Hydrolysis by water converts imine ions to ketone and ammonia."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_011.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00013",
              "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00013"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00012",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00012-alt",
                  "text": "The conversion reaction of benzonitrile to propiophenone by reacting with ethylmagnesium bromide (Grignard reagent) in ether followed by acidic hydrolysis."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_012.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "example",
              "id": "exam-00001",
              "title": {
                "segmentId": "m00236:example-title:exam-00001-title",
                "text": "Synthesizing a Ketone from a Nitrile"
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "para",
                  "id": "para-00014",
                  "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00014"
                },
                {
                  "type": "para",
                  "id": "para-00015",
                  "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00015",
                  "title": {
                    "segmentId": "m00236:para-title:para-00015-title",
                    "text": "Strategy"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "para",
                  "id": "para-00016",
                  "segmentId": "m00236:para:para-00016",
                  "title": {
                    "segmentId": "m00236:para-title:para-00016-title",
                    "text": "Solution"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00013",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00013-alt",
                  "text": "The condensed structure of 2-methyl-3-pentanone. The third carbon is double-bonded to an oxygen atom. A methyl group is attached to the second carbon."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_013.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00014",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00236:alt:fig-00014-alt",
                  "text": "The preparation of 2-methyl-3-pentanone by the reaction of nitrile and Grignard reagent. The first pathway used propionitrile and isopropylmagnesium bromide. The second pathway used isobutyronitrile and ethylmagnesium bromide."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_20_07_014.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
